# Project structure

## Feature-based layout

Group code by feature, not by file type. Shared code lives in top-level folders only when two or more features use it.

```text
src/
├── app/                 # App shell: providers, router, global styles (Expo Router: the app/ folder at the root)
├── features/
│   └── users/
│       ├── api.ts           # Endpoints and query hooks for this feature
│       ├── types.ts         # Schemas and types
│       ├── components/      # Components used only by this feature
│       ├── hooks/
│       ├── utils/
│       └── index.ts         # The feature's public API
├── components/          # Shared, generic UI (Button, Modal, TextField)
├── hooks/               # Shared hooks (useDebounce, useMediaQuery)
├── lib/                 # Configured third-party clients (apiClient, queryClient, i18n)
├── utils/               # Pure shared helpers (formatDate, assertNever)
├── constants/
├── types/               # Truly global types only
└── test/                # Test setup, MSW handlers, render helpers, factories
```

## Boundaries

- A feature may import from shared folders (`components`, `hooks`, `lib`, `utils`) and from another feature's `index.ts` only.
- Never import another feature's internals (`features/users/components/…` from `features/orders`).
- Shared folders never import from `features/`.
- No circular imports. ESLint's `import-x/no-cycle` catches them.
- Keep `index.ts` barrels at the feature level only, and be intentional: export the feature's public API, not everything. Deep barrels in every folder slow down Vite and Metro and hide cycles.

## Imports

- Use the `@/` alias for anything outside the current feature: `import { Button } from '@/components/Button'`. Use relative imports inside a feature.
- Order: built-ins and packages, then `@/` aliases, then relative, then styles. ESLint sorts them.
- `import type` for types.
- No default exports in shared code, so names stay the same everywhere.

## File naming

| File                       | Name                                            |
| -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Component                  | `UserCard.tsx`                                  |
| Component test             | `UserCard.test.tsx`                             |
| Hook                       | `useUser.ts`                                    |
| Utility                    | `formatPrice.ts`                                |
| Types / schemas            | `types.ts` or `user.schema.ts`                  |
| Constants                  | `constants.ts`                                  |
| Styles (CSS Modules)       | `UserCard.module.css`                           |
| Platform-specific (native) | `Map.ios.tsx`, `Map.android.tsx`, `Map.web.tsx` |

Tests, stories, styles and types sit next to the file they belong to. Colocation beats deep `__tests__` trees. A shared component can get its own folder (`components/Button/Button.tsx`, `Button.test.tsx`, `Button.module.css`).

Types are `PascalCase` with no `I` or `T` prefix (`User`, not `IUser` or `TUser`). One file exports one primary thing; small helpers can live beside it until they grow.

## Size limits

- Files: about 300 lines. Functions: about 120 lines. ESLint enforces both (tests are excluded).
- Complexity: 10 per function. Nesting depth: 4.
- Above that, split by responsibility, not by line count.

## Config and environment

- Read env vars in one module (`lib/env.ts`) that parses them with a schema and exports a typed object. Nowhere else reads `import.meta.env` or `process.env`.
- Feature flags come from one place too.
